<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="5ee51558">&lt;p&gt;
    A charter is a document that reflects the client&apos;s agreement and authorization of the project scope, approach,
    timeline, resources, governance, and other major factors. (Note: Project Charters are scalable. For smaller projects,
    the charter could be only a single page where for a large program it could be multiple pages.)&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    After updating the initial planning and establishing the project organization and governance structure, the Project
    Charter is updated. The project charter is a summary form of the project in a format that allows project participants
    and stakeholders to understand:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Who&lt;/strong&gt; – Who authorized the project? Who is involved in the project?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;What&lt;/strong&gt; – What is the intended outcome?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Why&lt;/strong&gt; – Why this project (client objectives)?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;When&lt;/strong&gt; – When will the project start and end?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;How&lt;/strong&gt; – How will the work be performed (project approach)?
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This information is critical because it confirms the baseline project plan and it is used to orient the project team to
    the project. It also provides a means of monitoring the project during the Execute phase.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    The Objectives, Deliverables, Workplan (ODW) method will contribute to charter development by translating the
    objectives into deliverables (made up of work products), which will in turn shape the workplan (e.g., workstreams,
    phases, phase gates and activities).&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    The ODW will maintain alignment and integrity of the workplan against the client’s needs to avoid the risk of producing
    a meaningless plan of unfocused activities. The collective information will feed into the project charter to forecast
    work products and timelines as performance measures to aid project monitoring.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Confirm client agreement of the charter and obtain sign off prior to or at project kickoff. If significant scope
    changes occur, it may be necessary to update the charter. The content of the charter must be aligned to the statement
    of work in the engagement agreement. If the charter contains additional elements, an amendment to the engagement
    agreement may need to be issued. Please also make sure that the local signature policies are followed. In case of
    doubt, please consult with the area or sub-area Q&amp;RM for your service line and the general counsel. A version
    history should be kept in the event there are significant scope changes that require updates or amendments.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
